    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kairouan was founded around 670 as an Arab military post far from the sea, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> (800 to 909) it became a capital and a centre of Islamic and secular learning.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> neglected it after 909 and the Zirids ruled again from it, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a> took it in 1057 and ended its standing as the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>.</li><li>The city began to recover under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a> in the 13th century, was taken by the French in 1881, and kept its Great Mosque, its basins and its place in Islamic tradition.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Local tradition holds that seven pilgrimages to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a> equal one pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and according to some this makes it the fourth holiest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

