    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 670</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> chooses the site of Kairouan for a military post</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">702</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihya">Al-Kahina</a> is killed and her army defeated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">742</span><span class="rf-text">Handhala ibn Safwan defeats the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> army near the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">800</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim ibn al-Aghlab</a> becomes emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> and makes Kairouan his capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">909</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> and neglect Kairouan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1057</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> take Kairouan from the Zirids and destroy it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">13th century</span><span class="rf-text">Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a> the city begins to emerge from its ruins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1881</span><span class="rf-text">The French take Kairouan and open it to non-Muslims</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kairouan was founded around 670, when the Arab general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a>, acting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> Mu'awiya, selected a site in the middle of a dense forest, then infested with wild beasts and reptiles, for a military post from which to conquer the West. A town called Kamounia had stood where Kairouan now stands, and it had housed a Byzantine garrison before the Arab conquest. The site lay far from the sea, and so was safe from the attacks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, who had fiercely resisted the Arab invasion.</p><p>The name is the Arabic al-Qayrawan, meaning a military group or a caravan, borrowed early from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> word karawan, which meant a military column or a caravan. In Berber the city was once called Tikirwan, thought to be an adaptation of the Arabic name. Early modern English sometimes wrote it Cairoan. The Great Mosque, the city's main landmark, was originally built when Kairouan was founded.</p><p>Berber resistance continued after the founding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusaila">Kusaila</a> led it first, and his troops killed Uqba at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biskra">Biskra</a> about fifteen years after the military post was established. A Berber woman called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dihya">Al-Kahina</a> then led it, until she was killed and her army defeated in 702. A mass conversion of the Berbers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab.jpg" alt="Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aghlabid_dirham_-_Ibrahim_ibn_al-Aghlab_-_al-Maamoun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DrFO.Jr.Tn, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 745 Kairouan was already a developed city with luxuriant gardens and olive groves. Its first great period began in 800, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> Harun ar-Rashid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim ibn al-Aghlab</a> as emir and hereditary ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>. The Aghlabid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled between 800 and 909, embellished Kairouan and made it their capital. It became famous for its wealth and prosperity, reaching the levels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> rebuilt the great mosque and established in it a university, a centre of education in Islamic thought and in the secular sciences, with a role comparable to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In the 9th century scholars came from across the Islamic world, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahnun">Sahnun</a> and Asad ibn al-Furat made the city a centre for spreading the Islamic sciences. The Aghlabids also built palaces, fortifications and waterworks, of which only the pools remain, and they conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 827.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> had moved on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and left the Zirids as their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in Ifriqiya, the Zirids governed again from Kairouan. Schools and universities flourished, overseas trade in local manufactures and farm produce ran high, and the courts of the Zirid rulers were centres of refinement.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/raqqada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raqqada.jpg" alt="Raqqada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bassins_Arabe_de_Raqada_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IssamBarhoumi, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kairouan began as a post of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, whose general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> founded it. In October 741, during the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-revolt">Berber Revolt</a>, the Ifriqiyan army and a Syrian force sent by the caliph were destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> at the Battle of Bagdoura. The Umayyad Caliph Hisham then appointed Handhala ibn Safwan governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and he reached Kairouan around April 742. In 745 Kharijite Berbers captured the city, and power struggles continued until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim ibn al-Aghlab</a> recaptured it at the end of the 8th century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabid dynasty</a>, which Ibrahim founded, ruled Ifriqiya between 800 and 909 with Kairouan as its capital. In 893 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> began the movement of the Shiite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> through the mission of Abdullah al <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, and in 909 the Fatimids overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Aghlabids. They lived first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a> and soon moved their capital to the newly built Al Mahdiyah on the east coast. They later moved east to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, leaving the Zirids as their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in Ifriqiya.</p><p>The Zirids declared their independence from Cairo and their conversion to Sunni Islam in 1045, by giving allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsids</a> ruled Ifriqiya in the 13th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a> later. The French took Kairouan in 1881.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 9th to 11th centuries Kairouan was one of the great centres of Islamic civilisation, with a reputation as a hotbed of scholarship across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. The Great Mosque was both a place of prayer and a centre for teaching the Islamic sciences under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> current. A tradition particular to Kairouan used Islamic law to enforce monogamy, by stipulating it in the marriage contract. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> festivals are held in the city in memory of saints.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were among the original settlers of Kairouan. Their community's golden era began in the late 8th century and lasted until the early 11th century, and for at least three generations it was a world centre of Talmudic and Halakhic scholarship. A Christian community existed during the early 11th century. Before the French arrived in 1881, non-Muslims were forbidden to live in the city.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Al-Idrisi</a>, writing in the 12th century, recorded that Kairouan was originally two cities, Kairouan itself and Sabra, which was the royal residence and at the height of its prosperity contained three hundred baths. He wrote that the city's water supply was scarce and that its people drank from a great cistern within the city. The Aghlabid basins of the 9th century, outside the ramparts, held a total of 68,800 cubic metres of water.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kairouan was repeatedly threatened during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-revolt">Berber revolts</a>. After the defeat at Bagdoura in 741, the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> lay open to the rebels. In 742 the qadi Abd al-Rahman ibn Oqba al-Ghaffari defended the city against an attack by the Sufrite leader Oqasha ibn Ayub al-Fezari. Handhala ibn Safwan then defeated Oqasha at El-Qarn and is said to have put the entire population of Kairouan under arms to face a second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> army said to number some 300,000.</p><p>In perhaps the bloodiest encounter of the Berber wars, Handhala defeated that army at al-Asnam in May 742, perhaps a little later, three miles outside the city. Some 120,000 to 180,000 Berbers, including their commander Abd al-Wahid ibn Yazid, fell in that single battle. Kharijite Berbers captured the city in 745.</p><p>The worst blow came after the Zirids broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1045. As punishment the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mustansir Billah sent the Arab tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a> to invade Ifriqiya. They took Kairouan from the Zirids in 1057 and destroyed it, so that it never regained its former importance. Their influx was a major factor in the spread of nomadism where agriculture had been dominant, and in most of the country the land was laid waste for nearly two centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Al-Idrisi</a> described the city in the 12th century as largely in ruins, with only a portion of it enclosed by an earthen wall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of Kairouan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_Kairouan_Panorama_-_Grande_Mosqu%C3%A9e_de_Kairouan_Panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MAREK SZAREJKO from CLONMEL, IRELAND - POLAND, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Great Mosque of Sidi-Uqba, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a>, is one of the largest Islamic monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and one of the oldest places of worship in the Islamic world. It was rebuilt substantially under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> in the 9th century and now occupies over 9,000 square metres. The Mosque of the Three Doors was founded in 866, and its facade carries three inscriptions in Kufic script. The Zawiya of Sidi Sahib, or Mosque of the Barber, holds the tomb of Abu Zama' al-Balawi, who according to legend kept three hairs of Muhammad's beard.</p><p>The Aghlabid basins are considered to be among the most important hydraulic systems in the history of the Muslim world. The French built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a> to Kairouan railway, which ran from 1882 to 1996. The old city and its monuments became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1988.</p><p>Kairouan is today the capital of the Kairouan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, and in 2014 it had about 187,000 inhabitants. The Jewish community returned only after Tunisia became a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1881 and had disappeared by the 1960s; its dilapidated cemetery remains. The city is also known for its pastries, such as zlebia and makroudh.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kairouan, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, was founded around 670 as an Arab military post. It was the Aghlabid capital and a centre of Islamic and Jewish learning until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> destroyed it in 1057, and its Great Mosque still stands.</em></p>

